A||||3||SteveJ||Upper end Caddy and Lexus colors||||5||
Z||000000||SteveJ||02-25-2001||07:11 PM||||Those upper end Caddy and Lexus colors that are kind of pearlesent white. How do they get that effect?||||reg||5
Z||000001||maninblack||02-25-2001||08:14 PM||||you can get the paint shop to paint those colors, but it is rather expensive.It call<BR>pearl paint, its comes in pearl white, blue, red etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DISenchanted||02-26-2001||02:46 AM||||Another way is just to add some pearlesence to the clear coat.  Start with a base color, shoot 2-3 coats. Then shoot one coat of clear.  Next coat add a little bit of pearl to the clear then shoot.  Finish with 1-2 more coats of clear.  That is how you get most of the color "change" effects.  (ex. Blue base coats with a green pearl)||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||02-26-2001||07:56 AM||||Pearl is made up of tiny mica flakes in the color specified. Dupont and Basf make it in a powder form, it's to be mixed with the clear component of the base coat. See, when you mix base coat there's a clear, well, translucent part of the whole mix and when making a pearl coat you simply mix specified amounts of the pearl powder with this base coat clear stuff. It's called binder. You're supposed to spray 2-3 coats of color then as many coats of pearl as you want to the desired effect is reached then spray clear over all of that. Some of the other paint companies might have different procedures, I can only speak of the ones that I have been trained on.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
